---
/**
 * Shared fixed-position background layer for index/about/contact/blog:
 * the WebGL nebula canvas (#gc), the 2D network/ships/satellites canvas
 * (#sc), the bloom + grain overlays, and a uniform darkening overlay
 * (#bg-dim) on top of them. Byte-identical across all four pages before
 * this extraction (confirmed via diff) — the markup/CSS live here now;
 * the ~2,165-line engine that drives the two canvases stays a separate
 * external script (src/scripts/nebula-engine.js), loaded per-page with
 * `<script is:inline src="...">` right after this component, since the
 * relative path depth differs between src/pages/*.astro and
 * src/pages/blog/index.astro.
 *
 * `#window`/`#screen` are NOT a wrapper around page content — they're a
 * small fixed-position stack that sits behind everything. Each page's
 * real content lives in sibling elements after this component, with its
 * own position:relative + higher z-index, layered visually on top.
 */
---

<div id="window">
  <div id="screen">
    <canvas id="gc" aria-hidden="true"></canvas>
    <canvas id="sc" aria-hidden="true"></canvas>
    <div id="bg-bloom" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <svg id="bg-grain" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="100%" height="100%" preserveAspectRatio="none" aria-hidden="true">
      <filter id="bg-grain-noise"><feTurbulence type="fractalNoise" baseFrequency="0.55" numOctaves="2" stitchTiles="stitch" seed="2"/><feGaussianBlur stdDeviation="0.45"/><feColorMatrix values="0 0 0 0 0.55  0 0 0 0 0.55  0 0 0 0 0.58  0.7 0 0 0 -0.15"/></filter>
      <rect width="100%" height="100%" filter="url(#bg-grain-noise)"/>
    </svg>
    <div id="bg-dim" aria-hidden="true"></div>
  </div>
</div>

<style is:global>
  #bg-bloom {
    position:absolute; inset:0; z-index:0; pointer-events:none;
    background: radial-gradient(ellipse at 50% 55%,
      rgba(60,90,160,0) 0%, transparent 100%);
    transition: none;
    mix-blend-mode: screen;
  }
  #bg-grain {
    position:absolute; inset:0; z-index:2;
    width:100%; height:100%;
    display:block;
    pointer-events:none;
    opacity:0.25;
  }
  /* Uniform darkening layer for the nebula/bloom base only. Deliberately
     BELOW #sc (z-index 1) — ships, satellites, stars and the constellation
     are all drawn on #sc, and CRAFT's brightness boost for them happens
     inside that canvas draw code. A dim layer sitting ABOVE #sc would
     re-darken everything CRAFT just brightened, with no way for CRAFT to
     compensate (it can't reach outside the canvas into a separate DOM
     layer). Keeping this below #sc means it only pushes the nebula/bloom
     glow darker, and never touches anything drawn on top of it. */
  #bg-dim {
    position:absolute; inset:0; z-index:0;
    pointer-events:none;
    background: rgba(0,0,0, var(--bg-dim-opacity, 0.35));
  }
  #window {
    position:fixed; inset:0;
    width:100vw; height:100vh;
    display:flex;
    flex-direction:column;
    overflow:hidden;
  }
  #screen {
    position:relative; flex:1; background:var(--c-bg); overflow:hidden;
  }
  #window::before {
    content:'';
    position:absolute; inset:0; z-index:200;
    pointer-events:none;
    background:repeating-linear-gradient(
      0deg,
      transparent, transparent 3px,
      rgba(0,0,0,0.05) 3px, rgba(0,0,0,0.05) 4px
    );
  }
  /* Explicit sizing (not just position:absolute) so these match their FINAL
     script-driven geometry from first paint, not just an intermediate size.
     Canvas's replaced-element default is 300x150 — without any CSS size,
     that's what paints first, then jumps when JS measures/positions it: a
     real, measured layout shift (Layout Instability API confirmed a 0.328
     CLS entry, #sc's previousRect at exactly 300x150).
     Fixing #gc to 100%/100% was enough — nebula-engine[-home].js's
     positionCanvases() sets its inline style to that exact same box
     (left:0;top:0;width:100%;height:100%), so there's nothing left for it
     to change.
     #sc needed more: positionCanvases() deliberately OVERSIZES it (65%
     larger than the viewport, centered via a negative offset — see
     nebula-engine.js's OVERSIZE constant — so craft can fly in from
     off-screen) and sets that via inline style, which beats this CSS
     rule's specificity regardless of paint timing. A first-paint size of
     100%/100% was still a real geometry change once that inline style
     landed — same shift, just from a different starting box. Matching the
     inline style's OX/OY/CW/CH math here (100% * 1.65, offset by
     -(0.65/2) = -32.5%) means the JS write becomes a no-op resize instead
     of a visible one. If OVERSIZE ever changes in the engine scripts,
     update these to match. */
  #gc { position:absolute; display:block; z-index:0; width:100%; height:100%; }
  #sc { position:absolute; display:block; z-index:1; pointer-events:none; left:-32.5%; top:-32.5%; width:165%; height:165%; }
</style>
